**user**:
> What is the entropy of a single fair coin toss?
**assistant**:
> The entropy \( H \) of a single fair coin toss can be calculated using the formula for entropy:
> 
> \[ H(X) = - \sum_{i} p(x_i) \log_b p(x_i) \]
> 
> For a fair coin toss, there are two possible outcomes: heads (\( H \)) and tails (\( T \)), with equal probabilities of \( p(H) = 0.5 \) and \( p(T) = 0.5 \).
> 
> Using base 2 for the logarithm:
> 
> \[ H(X) = - [0.5 \log_2(0.5) + 0.5 \log_2(0.5)] \]
> 
> \[ = - [0.5 \cdot (-1) + 0.5 \cdot (-1)] \]
> 
> \[ = - [(-0.5) + (-0.5)] \]
> 
> \[ = 1 \]
> 
> So, the entropy of a single fair coin toss is 1 bit. This means that a single fair coin toss carries 1 bit of information.
